                        {
                          "String": "SURFACE WATER: Stearic acid was identified in samples of water taken from the Po and Lambro Rivers in Italy, Sept 1988 to Jan 1990(1). Water samples collected in England from the River Irwell, River Etherow, and River Mersey in 1984 had average stearic acid concentrations of 12.55, 3.05, and 2.10 ug/L, respectively(2). Samples from the Delaware River north of Philadelphia, PA in Aug 1977 had stearic acid concentrations ranging from moderate to very high(3). Ten replicate water samples collected at the Inner Harbor Navigation Canal near Lake Pontchartain, New Orleans, LA on June 23, 1980 contained an average of 0.6 ppb stearic acid from the flood tide and was not detected from the ebb tide(4). Stearic acid has been identified, not quantified, in a study of 250 samples collected from the Fox River System in Wisconsin from 1976 to 1977(5). Large mass water samples were collected from the Sacramento River, Sacramento County, CA on Dec 10, 1993, from Putah Creek, Yolo County, CA on Nov 26, 1993, and from Clear Lake, CA on April 5, 1994; stearic acid was detected in Clear Lake water at unknown concentrations(6). Stearic acid was not detected in a British lowland river and a West German lowland river(7).",
