                    "Value": {
                      "StringWithMarkup": [
                        {
                          "String": "An average stearic acid concentration of 355 ng/uL was identified in an industrial wastewater survey in which samples collected between Nov 1, 1979 to Nov 1, 1981 were analyzed(1). Water samples from three secondary treatment publicly owned treatment works in NJ were found to contain average stearic acid concentrations of 9.5, 1.2, and 12 ppb(2). Secondary effluents from ten municipal and industrial wastewater treatment plants discharging into Illinois rivers were sampled; stearic acid was identified in effluents from one site (Addison Public Owned Treatment Works)(3). Stearic acid was found in straw pulp bleaching and combined mill effluents taken during a production week starting on May 20, 1983 in Denmark(4). The average concentration of stearic acid identified in Los Angeles County, CA effluent collected quarterly from November 1980 to August 1981 was determined to be 1,000 ug/L(5). Stearic acid was found in trace amounts from municipal landfill leachates in Gryta, Sweden(6). Wastewater from two kraft paper mills in GA sampled in March 1972 contained approximately 0.061 and 0.073 mg/l stearic acid (as the methyl derivative)(7). Water samples taken from the effluent of a kraft paper mill located on the shore of Lake Saimaa, Lappeenranta, Finland was found to contain a stearic acid concentration ranging from 5 to 480 ug/L over a 9 day period(8). Stearic acid was identified in Iona Island Sewage Treatment Plant(British Columbia, Canada) sewage and sludge effluent, concentrations ranging from 240 to 728 ug/L(9). Stearic acid, as the methyl derivative, has been identified in sewage treatment effluent water samples from the lower River Lee, North of London, England in large quantities (> 0.1 ug/L)(10). Effluents from two pulp mills located downstream of the Township of Kawerau, in the Eastern Bay of Plenty region, New Zealand were found to contain average concentrations of 196 and 8.5 ppb stearic acid(11). Samples collected from the Prestwich Sewage Treatment Plant effluent in 1984 had average stearic acid concentrations of 8.65 ug/L(12). Stearic acid has been identified in leachate samples collected from the interior of a municipal landfill situated in Gryta, Vasteras, Sweden in May 1990(13). Stearic acid has been identified in the influent (41.00 ug/L) and effluent (0.80 ug/L) from the Kallby STP located by the Hoje River, south of Lund, Sweden (14).",
