                    "Value": {
                      "StringWithMarkup": [
                        {
                          "String": "URBAN/SUBURBAN: Sampling of particulate matter and gaseous pollutants was conducted for three weeks between October 7 and 29, 1976 in Belgium; stearic acid was identified in the gas phase of urban air samples with an average concentration of 2.27 ug/1000 cu m and in particulate samples with an average concentration of 35.70 ug/1000 cu m(1). Aerosol samples collected in the Tsukuba area, northeast of Tokyo, Japan on April 28-29, 1985 contained stearic acid in unknown concentrations(3). Aerosol samples were collected systematically throughout a complete annual cycle (1982) at four urban sites in southern California. Ambient annual concentrations of stearic acid ranged from 41.1 to 59.2 ng/cu m(4). Stearic acid had an average concentration of 33.8 ng/cu m in 4 urban sites from southern CA from samples taken Sept 8-9, 1993(5). Stearic acid was found in Vienna, Austria at 66 ng/cu m from samples taken June 1997(6). A southern CA atmospheric study gave concentrations of 25.9, 106, 29.5, 27.7 and 52.2 ng/cu m for Long Beach, Mira Loma, Riverside, San Dimas and Upland, respectively(7). Atmospheric samples taken Dec 26-28, 1995 and Jan 4-6, 1996 in Fresno, CA had 60.0 and 26.6 ng/cu m and samples taken in Bakersfield had 24.1 and 38.3 ng/cu m, respectively(8). Stearic acid was found in atmospheric samples from North Birmingham, AL, Jefferson Street, GA, Gulfport, MS, Pensacola, FL and OLF#8, FL (a suburban site 20 km northwest of downtown Pensacola) at concentrations of 11.9, 6.33, 0.74, 4.42 and 0.34 ng/cu m, respectively(9). Stearic acid was detected at a mean concentration of 115 ng/m cu, with range of 51-181 ng/m cu, in air samples collected between October and December of 2003 at seven locations in Hong Kong(10).",
